--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: edad3068bd72c46f0a544237e3cec695, type: 3}
  m_Name: Chapter_6
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::ChapterData
  chapterNumber: 6
  chapterTitle: First Day Back
  timelineText: Six months after the birth
  storyText: Your baby is six months old, and today you go back to work. You were
    up before dawn to feed and express, and the milk still needs labelling. The nappy
    bag isn't packed, the carer needs a handover list, and you want to check in with
    her before you leave. None of your work clothes fit the way they used to. And
    every time you look at the baby, the guilt about leaving pulls at you. Get ready
    for work and get out the door.
  weekContext: Month 4, postpartum
  obstacleText: Guilt and separation anxiety about leaving the baby
  mainTaskText: Getting ready for work
  contentWarningText: This game explores the emotional and physical strain of pregnancy
    and early parenthood. You can exit at any time.
  showMeterIntro: 0
  showContentWarning: 0
  meterIntroNote: 'Move with the arrow keys or WASD, and press E to use things. While
    getting ready for work, hold E and respond to the prompts: hold SPACE to get
    herself ready, tap Q to push past the clothes that don''t fit, and tap F to quiet
    the worry loop.'
  musicClip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: d45b17dd9d6da4113bdea9e7d56dbc7b, type: 3}
  ambienceClip: {fileID: 0}
  hourTransitionText: She's out the door. Part of her isn't.
  bodyDrainStory: She'd been up feeding and expressing before the day began, then
    rushing to get out the door.
  mindDrainStory: The handover list, the carer, the nappy bag and work all pulled
    at her at once.
  personDrainStory: Between being a mum and going back to work, there wasn't a moment
    where she was just herself.
  maskDrainStory: She had to look calm and ready for work while the guilt about leaving
    the baby sat just underneath.
  partnerHourSeconds: 120
  baseDrain:
    body: 0.02
    mind: 0.035
    feelingLikeAPerson: 0.035
    mask: 0.035
  choices:
  - label: Sit down and have a moment
    multipliers:
      body: 1
      mind: 0.6
      feelingLikeAPerson: 1
      mask: 1.4
  - label: Push on and get out the door
    multipliers:
      body: 1
      mind: 1.5
      feelingLikeAPerson: 1
      mask: 0.6
  choiceTriggerTaskId: get_ready
  mainTaskHitMeter: 2
  mainTaskHitFraction: 0.5
  responses:
  - label: Take over the baby admin
    nextChapterEase:
      body: 1
      mind: 1
      feelingLikeAPerson: 1
      mask: 1
    impactText: You buy the nappies and answer the carer about food portions yourself.
      Tomorrow she can be at work without running the house from her phone.
    learnText: In a study of mothers, most said they alone were responsible for running
      household routines, like organising schedules. Feeling solely responsible was
      linked with lower wellbeing and lower satisfaction with their partner.
    helpText: Own whole areas, like the nappy supply, the carer and drop-offs, including
      the remembering. Agree together who is in charge of what, so she doesn't have
      to keep track of it all.
    sourceText: Ciciolla & Luthar (2019), Sex Roles 81:467-86
  - label: Ask how she's really feeling
    nextChapterEase:
      body: 1
      mind: 1
      feelingLikeAPerson: 1
      mask: 1
    impactText: You say you know leaving the baby was hard, and ask how she really
      is. She tells you about crying in the car park, and the relief she felt guilty
      for.
    learnText: Mothers returning to work after leave describe guilt, abandonment
      and loneliness at leaving their child, and often guilt is the first feeling.
      Many also feel joy at getting their working life back.
    helpText: Ask about her day, not just the job. Make room for both the guilt and
      the happiness, without judging either.
    sourceText: Rapisarda et al. (2024), Arch Womens Ment Health 27:607-18
  - label: Make the evening easier
    nextChapterEase:
      body: 1
      mind: 1
      feelingLikeAPerson: 1
      mask: 1
    impactText: You cook dinner and do the bath and bedtime, so the evening isn't
      a second shift for her.
    learnText: Practical support, good communication and relationship satisfaction
      were each linked with less depression for mothers in a review of 120 published
      studies.
    helpText: 'Plan the evenings together before her first week back: who cooks,
      and who does bath and bedtime. Batch cooking on the weekend saves time on work
      nights.'
    sourceText: Pilkington et al. (2015), J Affect Disord 178:165-80
  - label: Carry on as normal
    nextChapterEase:
      body: 1
      mind: 1
      feelingLikeAPerson: 1
      mask: 1
    impactText: Work was fine, she says. You leave it there, and she does bedtime
      on her own after a full day.
    learnText: When mothers carry the household load alone, research links it with
      lower wellbeing and lower satisfaction with their partner, even when the relationship
      is otherwise close.
    helpText: Don't wait to be asked. Notice the traces, like the work bag by the
      door, the note from the carer and the empty nappy box, and take one over tonight.
    sourceText: Ciciolla & Luthar (2019), Sex Roles 81:467-86
  learnHeading: Going back to work
  selfHourNotice: 'The evening after her first day back. She says: "Work was fine."'
